Chapter: DtlsRandomTest

  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Net chapter DtlsEndpoint

 `ProtocolStack.md`: `dtls-ep-new` takes the Hello `Random` from the caller and
 stores the value as `ep-random`, and `dtls-ep-random` reads that field. The
 capability was in the signature and had NO CALLER anywhere in the tree, so
 nothing had ever shown the caller's bytes reaching the wire -- the state
 `ChainCore`'s hash was in until an arm ran it (L-UNHEARD).

 The arm the design asks for: two endpoints built from DIFFERENT caller entropy
 must answer different `Random` values, and each must answer the caller's own
 bytes rather than anything derived from the key.

 A single endpoint proves nothing here. An implementation deriving `Random` from
 the endpoint's public key answers a stable 32 bytes and looks correct until a
 second endpoint is asked, which is why both are built from ONE private key and
 differ only in the entropy handed in.

Section: Fixtures

  ramp : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  ramp (n) (base) (acc) = if n <= 0 then acc else ramp (n - 1) base (acc & [base + n])

  key-bytes : Integer -> List Integer
  key-bytes (dummy) = ramp 32 100 []

  ep-with : List Integer -> DtlsEp
  ep-with (random) = dtls-ep-new False (key-bytes 0) random [9, 9, 9, 9] [10, 0, 0, 1]

  first-four : List Integer -> Text
  first-four (xs) =
    if list-length xs < 4 then "short"
    else show (list-at xs 0) & "," & show (list-at xs 1) & "," & show (list-at xs 2) & "," & show (list-at xs 3)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
    let ra = ramp 32 0 []
    in let rb = ramp 32 200 []
    in let ea = ep-with ra
    in let eb = ep-with rb
    in let ga = dtls-ep-random ea
    in let gb = dtls-ep-random eb
    in act
      print-line-uni ("endpoint a Random starts " & first-four ga & ", length " & show (list-length ga))
      print-line-uni ("endpoint b Random starts " & first-four gb & ", length " & show (list-length gb))
      print-line-uni ("a carries the caller's own first byte: " & show (list-at ga 0 == list-at ra 0))
      print-line-uni ("b carries the caller's own first byte: " & show (list-at gb 0 == list-at rb 0))
      print-line-uni ("two endpoints from one key differ: " & show (list-at ga 0 /= list-at gb 0))
    end
  end
